WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.380
It's been nearly four decades since its release,
but John Carpenter's The Thing still remains

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.050
one of the greatest horror films of all time.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:13.710
As renowned as it is for its creature designs,
lead performances and deeply unsettling atmosphere,

00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:18.760
it's also famous for its ending - which leaves
more than its fair share of unanswered questions.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.520
The film opens with the classic image of a
flying saucer barrelling through space toward

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:25.540
Earth.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:30.280
Much later, we learn that the craft had crashed
on our planet in Antarctica - and, according

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:35.250
to Norris, it might have been been buried
in the ice for 100,000 years.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:39.379
That's virtually all we get of the Thing's
origin story, and, like so much else in the

00:00:39.379 --> 00:00:42.540
film, it poses more questions than it answers.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:47.379
After all, that ship looked pretty sophisticated,
which suggests that it was built by an alien

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:50.609
race of considerable technological skill.

00:00:50.609 --> 00:00:54.889
If that's the case, it's entirely possible
that more have been built, meaning it's also

00:00:54.889 --> 00:00:59.649
possible that other similar ships made their
way to Earth at some point or another.

00:00:59.649 --> 00:01:02.179
Have any of them crashed, or even landed?

00:01:02.179 --> 00:01:06.830
Is there a whole fleet out there, just waiting
for a sign that there’s life on our planet?

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:11.690
Or was the ship's crash landing the result
of a hideous alien hijacking?

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:16.170
It's impossible to tell for sure, but the
presence of that ship hangs over the whole

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.170
movie.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:20.110
“It could have imitated a million life forms
on a million planets.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.660
It could change into any one of them at any
time.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:24.400
Now it wants life-forms on Earth.”

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.880
Even if it had been in some kind of stasis,
the alien spacecraft which contained the Thing

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:35.110
had been present on our planet for millennia,
but even with all of that time on Earth, the

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:38.790
movie doesn’t give us the creature’s entire
history, either.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:43.710
It's possible that all this has happened before,
either with another spacecraft, or with the

00:01:43.710 --> 00:01:46.250
same spacecraft encountered by other people.

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:52.090
A lot has changed in 100,000 years, and the
spacecraft may not have always been completely

00:01:52.090 --> 00:01:53.780
buried in the ice.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:58.280
What if there’s alien DNA out in the world
right now, spread by somebody who encountered

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.920
it many years ago?

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.050
Part of the film’s effectiveness comes from
the fact that it leaves those questions unanswered,

00:02:04.050 --> 00:02:09.299
and dials up the paranoia over just what the
Thing is and what it can do.

00:02:09.299 --> 00:02:14.150
But what we do know is that it can infiltrate
mankind and assimilate seamlessly if it wants

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.640
to - so who's to say it hasn't already?

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:22.880
As the paranoia over who might be infected
by the Thing sets in, the men of the station

00:02:22.889 --> 00:02:25.130
quickly begin to turn on each other.

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:30.639
This conflict hits a fever pitch when Nauls
tries to leave MacReady out in the storm after

00:02:30.639 --> 00:02:35.560
finding his torn jacket outside - which suggests
that he may have been attacked at some point.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.469
“Childs, what if we’re wrong about him?!”

00:02:38.469 --> 00:02:40.129
“Well then we’re wrong!”

00:02:40.129 --> 00:02:45.680
MacReady eventually makes his way back inside
and ultimately takes command of the survivors,

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:49.319
but this seemingly vital plot point is quickly
forgotten.

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:52.120
Is there a logical reason for it to have been
there?

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:57.060
Is it really proof that Mac was already assimilated,
and that the organisms controlling him are

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.849
just really good at hiding it - or is something
else going on?

00:03:00.849 --> 00:03:05.620
The film never answers that question, but
it does feed into the famous final scene in

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:06.880
a major way.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:12.079
The film’s iconic blood test sequence raises
another interesting question about the nature

00:03:12.079 --> 00:03:13.279
of the Thing.

00:03:13.279 --> 00:03:18.480
Palmer is the one who turns out to be infected,
and we see his blood basically jump out of

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:21.739
its petri dish when confronted with a hot
wire.

00:03:21.739 --> 00:03:26.379
We also know that the Thing will divide into
different parts that will act independently

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:31.310
if they need to, and that it can infect people
on a microbiotic level.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:36.469
Does all of this mean that infected blood
can in some way act, or at least survive,

00:03:36.469 --> 00:03:37.469
on its own?

00:03:37.469 --> 00:03:41.950
There's no short supply of blood scattered
throughout the film, both in the Norwegian

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:44.529
station and among the Americans.

00:03:44.529 --> 00:03:50.680
If it's somehow able to retain the alien organism
within it, it could at the very least survive

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:51.719
out in the cold.

00:03:51.719 --> 00:03:57.069
If it's able to transfer itself to other tissue
it comes into contact with, it could do even

00:03:57.069 --> 00:03:58.239
more than that.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:02.920
Fire can destroy most things, but it can't
eliminate every last drop of blood.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:06.200
The Thing’s ending is famously ambiguous.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:11.059
After destroying the station, MacReady stumbles
outside with a bottle of scotch in hand to

00:04:11.059 --> 00:04:16.010
find Childs returning, claiming his absence
from the final struggle was because he had

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:17.440
left to look for Blair.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:19.440
“If you’re worried about me-”

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:25.740
“If we’ve got any surprises for each other,
I don’t think we’re in much shape to do

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:26.840
anything about it.”

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.590
This moment has led to a number of fan theories
hypothesizing which of the two men might be

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:32.590
infected.

00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:36.560
It could be either Mac or Childs - or even
both.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:43.430
“Why don’t we just wait here for a little
while.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:46.560
See what happens.”

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:51.230
Some fans claim Mac's bottle isn't filled
with Scotch, but gasoline, and that he tricked

00:04:51.230 --> 00:04:55.630
Childs into proving he isn't human by making
him drink it.

00:04:55.630 --> 00:05:00.360
Others believe that his little smile at the
end could mean that Mac is the Thing.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:04.610
The closest thing to a definitive answer may
have come from cinematographer Dean Cundey,

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:09.100
who said he and Carpenter used eye light earlier
in the film to suggest who was human and who

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:10.170
wasn't.

00:05:10.170 --> 00:05:15.340
If you look closely, that could well mean
it’s likely that the Thing is living in

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:16.520
Childs.

00:05:16.520 --> 00:05:21.360
Whether it’s Childs or MacReady, there’s
a strong implication at the end of the film

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:27.090
that at least one of the two men remains infected
by the Thing - which suggests that the alien’s

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:28.640
story is far from over.

00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:30.270
So what happens now?

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:34.920
If only one of them is the Thing, it's quite
likely it will eventually assimilate the other

00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:35.920
human body.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:39.330
Could the Thing then hold out long enough
to wait for help?

00:05:39.330 --> 00:05:43.880
Could they feign death and eventually assimilate
other, healthier humans?

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:48.400
Could they even let the ice reclaim them and
return to the same kind of stasis from which

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.130
the Thing apparently came?

00:05:50.130 --> 00:05:55.100
Whatever the case may be, this story clearly
doesn’t end with Childs and MacReady - and

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:59.150
the version of Earth the film depicts could
well be doomed.

